"""Short, task-specific criteria for every editable input in the guided workspace."""
HELP={
'Enable AI assistance (optional)':'Selected by default. Start the example or your CSV analysis to unlock calculations; provider requests still require an explicit AI action or question. AI explains computed outputs, challenges assumptions and explores calculated scenarios; it does not receive raw rows or IDs. Business changes require confirmation. You can turn AI off and use the full deterministic workflow.',
'Planned share assigned to treatment':'Use the allocation specified before the experiment. A planned 50/50 split is 0.50. Do not replace it with the observed split to hide an assignment warning.',
'Choose your data':'Start with the example to learn the workflow. Upload a complete assigned-user CSV when you are ready; do not upload only purchasers or exposed users.',
'Upload the complete assigned-user export':'Include unit_id, variant and the primary numeric outcome. Retain assigned users with observed zero activity; missing measurements must remain missing.',
'Initiative name':'Use a recognizable product change, such as Checkout simplification. This becomes the title of the exported decision document.',
'Experiment ID':'Use a stable identifier for the same experiment across assumption updates. Saved versions are grouped under this ID; use a different ID for a different experiment.',
'Which outcome was this experiment intended to improve?':'Select the outcome defined in the experiment plan. Switching outcomes after looking at results makes that exploration post hoc; it does not establish a new confirmed primary win.',
'Which label is the current/control experience?':'Select the assignment representing the existing experience. The other arm will be compared against it; choosing the wrong label reverses the effect.',
'How is the outcome measured?':'Auto-detect recognizes 0/1 indicators versus numeric values. Choose Ratio of totals only when you have separate per-user numerator and denominator columns.',
'What is the denominator?':'For spend per session, select sessions and use spend as the numerator. The method compares arm totals; do not supply an already aggregated ratio as the denominator.',
'The numerator cannot exceed the denominator':'Tick for a bounded count ratio, such as clicks out of impressions. Leave unticked for spend per session, where a monetary numerator can exceed the session count.',
'Which direction helps users or the business?':'Higher is usually better for conversion; lower for latency or error rate. This determines benefit and harm throughout the posterior and economic model.',
'What business outcome does this metric represent?':'Choose the actual purpose, not just its column name. The AI uses this to distinguish growth, retention, engagement, monetization and user-experience tradeoffs.',
'Metric definition (optional but useful for AI)':'Explain the event, denominator, observation window and what zero means. Example: any completed purchase within seven days of assignment.',
'What relative improvement would make this worthwhile? (%)':'Choose a business-relevant goal before interpreting results. A 5% improvement takes conversion from 8% to 8.4%, or latency from 200 ms to 190 ms. It is not a significance cutoff.',
'Planning reference for the current metric value':'Relative improvement is undefined against zero. Supply an independently meaningful planning reference and recognize that the goal becomes conditional on it.',
'Confirm: users were assigned independently; the export includes all assignments and complete outcome windows; this is the planned fixed-horizon analysis.':'Confirm only after checking with the experiment owner. Clustered assignment, filtered exports or unplanned repeated stopping need methods this demo does not implement.',
'Attributes measured before assignment':'Select genuine baseline attributes only. Do not choose outcomes caused by treatment. These are optional diagnostics and do not automatically adjust the primary effect.',
'Use independently sourced historical conversion evidence':'Leave off unless comparable evidence from before/outside this experiment is available. Current control outcomes cannot be reused as independent prior evidence.',
'Historical conversion rate (%)':'Use the conversion rate from a comparable independent period/population. Enter 8 for 8%, not 0.08. A different population may not justify a strong prior.',
'Weight of that evidence (equivalent users per arm)':'How strongly should the historical rate influence the result? Use a small value for uncertain transfer; a large value can dominate small experiments. Do not optimize it for a favorable result.',
'Source and period of the independent evidence':'Identify the dataset or prior experiment and dates. This records provenance; a source description does not prove comparability or independence.',
'This source does not reuse outcomes from this experiment.':'Tick only when the historical evidence is genuinely separate. Reusing current data as a prior would count evidence twice.',
'Prior plausible effect scale (% of current value)':'Describe plausible differences before this experiment, in relative terms. The default is five times your useful-effect goal. Widen/narrow using domain evidence, not observed uplift.',
'Which change would be harmful?':'Choose the harmful direction from the metric definition: more crashes is harmful, fewer successful tasks may be harmful. The AI cannot edit this setting.',
'Largest tolerable relative deterioration (%)':'Use the maximum harm the product can accept, declared independently of results. If the baseline crash rate is 2%, a 20% relative increase means 2.4%, not 22%.',
'Current guardrail is zero: specify a direct deterioration limit':'A percentage of zero cannot define tolerance. Specify a direct bound from the metric definition and safety requirements, not whichever bound the result can pass.',
'Investigate one baseline attribute':'Choose an attribute to understand more deeply. All groups in the overview remain in the multiple-testing family; choosing this view does not shrink that family.',
'Early-window outcome':'Select the metric measured in the earlier window for every assigned user, including zeros. It must have the same definition and window length as the later outcome.',
'Late-window outcome':'Select a comparable later outcome on the same assigned users. Do not compare a three-day metric with a seven-day total or only users still active later.',
'These are equal-duration, complete windows of the same outcome for every assigned user.':'Confirm matched users, comparable definitions and equal observed time. Otherwise a timing contrast could reflect window length or survivor selection rather than treatment dynamics.',
'Future population attribute':'Choose a pre-treatment partition with credible external target shares and representation in both arms. A hypothetical mix is a scenario, not a validated forecast.',
'Use this hypothetical future mix for the economic scenarios':'Tick only if you want the edited target shares, rather than the experiment sample mix, to feed the financial model. It assumes effects transport within groups and disables unplanned stratified EVSI.',
'Also compare a gradual launch to part of the audience':'Off by default. Enable only if a smaller initial launch is a feasible choice for the team. It adds a separate budget and a fraction of the intended recipients to both the comparison and EVSI.',
'Total eligible people per month':'Count all people who could validly receive this product change before rollout coverage. Do not count only expected purchasers. This is multiplied once by Expected affected share; if your number already counts recipients, use 100% there.',
'Total eligible denominator units per month':'For a ratio, enter future denominator volume before coverage (for example sessions/month), not a user count. It is multiplied by affected share and the ratio effect.',
'Contribution per extra purchase (€)':'Use incremental contribution after variable costs per additional purchase, not gross order revenue. Ask finance if unknown; the starting value is hypothetical.',
'Contribution per extra outcome unit (€)':'Translate the selected outcome to incremental contribution. For revenue, a 30% contribution margin is 0.30 euros per extra revenue euro. Avoid counting revenue twice.',
'One-off global launch budget (€)':'Include engineering, QA and launch costs that happen once. Exclude ongoing maintenance entered separately. Use a documented estimate; do not lower it simply to make launch look attractive.',
'Ongoing maintenance per year (€)':'Use expected annual engineering/support cost of keeping the feature running. The engine converts it to monthly cost and charges only the comparison period. Example: €15,000/year becomes €1,250/month.',
'Budget to iterate and retest (€)':'Always state the budget for redesigning and evaluating a different version. The current experiment cannot predict that version’s uplift, so this budget is shown without inventing an investment return.',
'Expected affected share (%)':'Of the total eligible population, what fraction will actually receive the changed experience after deployment? 40% of 100,000 means 40,000 recipients/month. This is not the conversion rate; do not dilute an ITT effect again for behavior already included in it.',
'How many months of impact should we compare?':'The common period over which benefits and maintenance accumulate, not the duration of the test. Choose a period finance can sensibly plan over; use the same period for all alternatives.',
'How should we weigh downside?':'Balanced gives financial losses extra weight; More cautious increases that penalty. Focus on expected money uses the ordinary mean. The resulting decision score is distinct from forecast profit.',
'A global launch can be rolled back':'Tick only if the changed product experience can be undone, for example using a tested feature flag. It does not mean the engineering spend is refundable.',
'Only consider reversible rollouts':'Enable when rollback is a real product requirement. This can exclude an irreversible global launch even if it has attractive expected value.',
'Start with this share of the intended recipients (%)':'This is a fraction of the recipients already defined by eligible population × affected share. If those are 40,000/month, a 25% gradual launch starts with 10,000—not 25% of all users again.',
'Gradual rollout budget (€)':'Use the separate technical cost of safely launching to a smaller audience. It is not automatically a proportional fraction of global launch cost.',
'Relative uncertainty in contribution (%)':'This is a relative spread, not a hard minimum/maximum or a confidence percentage. About €2 standard deviation around a €10 mean is 20%. Use finance/history where possible; otherwise label it hypothetical.',
'Relative uncertainty in implementation cost (%)':'A 20% spread around a €10,000 mean is roughly €2,000 standard deviation, not a strict cap. Use the engineering estimate’s uncertainty rather than tuning for a desired recommendation.',
'Additional users in each arm':'New independently assigned users, in addition to the current sample. Enter a feasible future study size per arm; 2,000 means about 4,000 total new users. This is not a target significance level.',
'Cost of collecting the new sample (€)':'Include incremental study operations, incentives and data-collection costs. Do not include launch costs already in the alternatives, or the separate delay cost twice.',
'Cost of waiting for this evidence (€)':'Price the opportunity lost while the study runs. Two weeks of delay is not itself a euro amount—ask the owner/finance for a defensible estimate. Zero means unpriced, not proven free.',
'Optional initiative/company context':'Explain user pain, metric definitions, constraints and sources. Click Save context to apply the notes, or Get an AI strategic review to save and use them together. No Enter required; Enter adds a new line. These notes inform interpretation without silently changing numeric assumptions.',
'Owner’s decision / remaining context (optional)':'Record the accountable human choice, conditions, owner and next action. It stays distinct from the model’s preference and is saved/exported with the report.',
'Compare current draft with saved version':'Select a prior version of this experiment to see changed assumptions, sources and modeled decisions. It does not overwrite either version.'}


def input_help(label):
    return HELP.get(label,'Use the original experiment definition or a documented business estimate. Keep uncertain assumptions explicit and review how this choice changes the results before committing.')
